*html
	<div class="ct-thanks"><p class="ct-thanks-eyebrow">Members only</p><p class="ct-thanks-head">Exclusive content for Clearer Thinking Plus members</p><p>You’re reading the full members-only version of this article. Thank you for being a member: your support funds our research and keeps our tools and articles free for everyone.</p></div>
	<h1 class="ct-title">The Supplement Paradox: Why are so many claims about supplements B.S.?</h1>
	<p class="ct-byline">By Spencer Greenberg and Travis M.</p>

*image: https://beehiiv-images-production.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/asset/file/180b9ee7-cf9b-4be1-a5e9-3f36ec6f4555/image4.png

*html
	<p>When you’re watching a magic trick, you know the effect is not real. You know that coins don’t <em>really </em>vanish into thin air and that women aren’t <em>really </em>sawn in half on stage. But the illusion can be compelling anyway, particularly when the source of the deception is hard to find. Some arguments are like this too; you might have good reason to believe the conclusion is false, and yet find yourself compelled by its logic and struggle to spot where it went wrong.</p>
	<p>When it comes to dietary supplements, here are two arguments that work like magic tricks; empirical evidence (some of which is discussed below) does not support their conclusions, but many people nevertheless find them very compelling and struggle to identify anything wrong in their reasoning. What do you make of them?</p>
	<div class="ct-quote"><strong>Argument 1:</strong> Nature didn't always provide an abundance of the various vitamins, minerals, and so on that we need to survive and thrive. Our ancient ancestors didn’t have the easy access that we do today. Therefore, shouldn’t the average person now be able to add supplements to their diet that improve wellbeing and health, even without some specific health concern that they’re targeting?</div>
	<div class="ct-quote"><strong>Argument 2:</strong> Modern society is vastly different from the societies that we inhabited during our evolutionary development. It stands to reason that those differences could mean we're missing out on some important things we used to have. Including in our diets. Fortunately, dietary supplements can provide things our modern environment is missing or has less of and that we would have gotten before. Therefore, shouldn't the average person now be able to add supplements to their diet that improve wellbeing and health, even without some specific health concern that they’re targeting?</div>
	<p>Indeed, arguments like these are all over the place and consumer behavior appears to endorse their conclusions. <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2026.19291">Roughly 60%</a> of adults in the US report taking supplements, and a <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.3390/nu16121830">nationally representative US survey</a> of 12,529 people found that:</p>
	<ul><li>37% of supplement takers reported using them to “improve overall health”,</li><li>35% reported using them to “maintain health”, and</li><li>and yet only 27% of the supplements people reported using were used on the advice of a health care professional.</li></ul>

*image: https://beehiiv-images-production.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/asset/file/ae51ee61-3759-440c-8a37-9568f597daf4/upload_ec04e78b1ff07d29.png
*html
	<p class="ct-caption"><em>Chart taken from survey results (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="link" href="https://doi.org/10.3390/nu16121830">source</a>)</em></p>

*html
	<p>Hence, the truth about supplements matters because it potentially impacts your health and because the supplement industry is massive. If supplements don't work as claimed, that's an enormous misuse of resources. An <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2022.9167">editorial in the Journal of the American Medical Association</a> claims that “people in the US spent an estimated almost $50 billion on dietary supplements” in 2021. There are many stores with whole walls of supplements. It’s easy to get swept up in all the marketing hype without deeply considering the question: Are they trick or treatment?</p>
	<p>In this article, we argue that the balance of evidence does <strong>not</strong> support the idea that most generally healthy, non-pregnant adults in the US should be taking untargeted nutritional supplements (e.g., vitamins, minerals) to improve or maintain their health.</p>
	<h2>What we’re <em>not </em>saying</h2>
	<p>Before we dive into critiquing the common arguments, it’s important to point out that we are not saying supplements are useless to all people in all places at all times. We’re specifically looking at non-pregnant adults, and two other important kinds of exceptions should be noted:</p>
	<ol><li>For many nutritional deficiencies, supplements genuinely are incredibly helpful. If you go a long enough time without vitamin C, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminC-HealthProfessional/">you will likely get scurvy</a>; a lack of vitamin A <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminA-HealthProfessional/">can lead to Night Blindness</a>, and a severe deficiency of Vitamin D can <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminD-HealthProfessional/">cause serious bone issues</a>. It's absolutely critical that you get enough of each of the essential vitamins and minerals. Importantly, though, this is a different question than whether most people in the US will benefit from untargeted supplementation.</li><li>There is robust evidence in favor of some supplements having positive effects for specific medical conditions or in some contexts. We discuss this more below, but for example, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2023.12876">folic acid supplementation can benefit</a> people who are pregnant or planning to become pregnant in the next month or so, and creatine (when paired with resistance training) is <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1519/JSC.0000000000004862">genuinely useful</a> if you're trying to improve muscle strength.</li></ol>
	<p>Our focus is the claim that most people should be on supplements because they produce positive effects in average adultpeople without deficiencies and who lack specific medical conditions that can be treated by those supplements. In other words, <strong>we’re criticizing the view that the general adult population should all be taking untargeted supplements to improve or maintain their health.</strong></p>
	<p>It’s important that we’re clear about this: if you're taking supplements and finding benefits, or your doctor has recommended you take them, we are not suggesting you should stop taking them.</p>
	<h2>The evidence of large trials</h2>
	<p>If you look at studies on supplements, you'll find that earlier trials tend to show a wide variety of different benefits of many supplements. But a common pattern of events goes like this: those early and promising-seeming studies (which may be smaller, target more specific demographics, or have otherwise-biased samples) motivate larger, more rigorous studies in the general population, but the larger trials don’t find the same results in healthy adults.</p>
	<p>We don’t have the space to list out all the cases where this has happened, so let’s focus on a classic example: Vitamin D. You can find promising early trials on specific populations showing that vitamin D can help with many different things, including <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1093/ajcn/85.6.1586">cancer</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1177/0004867412465022">depression</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1359/jbmr.2003.18.2.343">falls</a>, and more. But when really large trials were conducted, these effects were not found for generally healthy adults. Examples include <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1056/nejmoa1809944">this study of vitamin D’s effects on cancer</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2020.10224">this one on depression</a>, and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1210/clinem/dgaa311">this one on falls</a>.</p>
	<p>There is evidence that darker-skinned individuals, those who don't get much sun, and anyone with low levels of vitamin D in their diet (e.g., vegans) may have <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminD-HealthProfessional/">an elevated risk of deficiency</a>, but that's still not grounds for a general population recommendation. And large-scale studies giving vitamin D supplements to the general population generally fail to find benefits for those without deficiency.</p>
	<p>In some cases, large trials revealed that supplements that initially seemed promising were actually likely to cause <em>harm </em>rather than benefit. We’ve <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.clearerthinking.org/post/how-to-tell-strong-evidence-from-weak-evidence">previously discussed</a> the fact that <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="http://doi.org/10.1007/BF00054304">early observational studies suggested</a> that diets rich in beta-carotene might help prevent lung cancer, but then <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="http://doi.org/10.1093/ajcn/62.6.1427S">larger and more robust studies</a> (and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0074558">later meta-analyses</a>) found that beta-carotene supplements apparently do the opposite: they appear to <em>increase </em>rates of lung cancer, especially for smokers and asbestos-exposed populations. The results were so concerning that <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="http://doi.org/10.1093/jnci/88.21.1550">one large trial</a> was stopped 21 months early because of “substantial evidence of possible harm.”</p>
	<p>This pattern of insufficient evidence of benefit applies to many supplements and many health concerns. Another example comes from the US Preventive Services Task Force, which recently conducted <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2021.15650">a large review of the evidence</a> and found that vitamin and mineral supplementation (including multivitamins) provided “little or no benefit” in preventing cardiovascular disease, cancer, or death in healthy adults. There was a slight reduction in cancer incidence with multivitamins, but <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/vitamin-supplementation-to-prevent-cvd-and-cancer-preventive-medication#fullrecommendationstart">the task force determined</a> this was insufficient to recommend their general untargeted use. They also explicitly warn <em>against</em> taking beta-carotene supplements to prevent cardiovascular disease or cancer, because the harms outweigh the benefits.</p>
	<p>There have been lots of studies of the effects of multivitamins (such as those in the review just mentioned, and others like <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2012.14805">this RCT</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1093/ajcn/nqac056">this RCT</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.3945/ajcn.112.049304">this meta-analysis</a>). The fact that such studies involve giving people lots of vitamins at once and yet they don’t find enough benefit to warrant recommending multivitamins for the general population of non-pregnant healthy adults suggests that (even though multivitamins have targeted uses for some people) the untargeted “spray and pray” approach to supplements is not effective.</p>
	<p>Again, we’re not claiming that nutritional supplements (e.g., vitamins and minerals) do <em>nothing</em>. They really can help some people who have specific health concerns or contexts, and people with genuine severe deficiencies must change their diet or add supplements. If you’re experiencing benefits from supplements or have been recommended supplements by your doctor, please do not stop using them on the basis of anything in this article.</p>
	<p>And, of course, there’s always a chance that there’s some benefit (or some harm) to generally healthy adults that hasn’t yet been discovered. We’re just arguing that the balance of evidence does not support the view that most average, healthy, non-pregnant adults should be taking supplements for untargeted improvements in or maintenance of their health.</p>
	<h2>What do the magic arguments get wrong?</h2>
	<p>What we’re calling “the supplement paradox” is the fact that there are compelling arguments to think that routine, untargeted dietary supplements could improve the health of average healthy adults (without nutritional deficiencies), and yet empirical research has not found the kinds of improvements that could justify such supplementation. We’re not going to solve the paradox by giving a deep diagnosis of all the failures of reasoning in those compelling arguments, but we do want to make a couple of observations about where they go wrong.</p>
	<h3>The first argument</h3>
	<p>Here’s a reminder of how the first argument goes:</p>
	<div class="ct-quote"><strong>Argument 1:</strong> Nature didn't always provide an abundance of the various vitamins, minerals, and so on that we need to survive and thrive. Our ancient ancestors didn’t have the easy access that we do today. Therefore, shouldn’t the average person now be able to add supplements to their diet that improve wellbeing and health, even without some specific health concern that they’re targeting?</div>
	<p>An assumption that can drive this kind of argument is: if something is needed by your body, then more of it is likely to be beneficial. But that assumption fails to consider alternatives, such as <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://bookshop.org/p/books/bad-science-quacks-hacks-and-big-pharma-flacks-ben-goldacre/00d54e9077f3bb7e?ean=9781429967099&amp;bkshp-astro=t">the ones mentioned by</a> the British physician, academic, and science writer Ben Goldacre:</p>
	<div class="ct-quote ct-italic">“Perhaps an excess of [supplement] is simply excreted, or turned into something else. Perhaps it just sits there doing nothing, because it’s not needed. After all, half a tank of petrol will get you across town just as easily as a full tank. Or perhaps, if you have an unusually enormous amount of [it] lying around in your body doing nothing, it doesn’t just do nothing. Perhaps it does something actively harmful. That would be a turn-up for the books, wouldn’t it?”</div>
	<h3><strong><em>The second argument</em></strong></h3>
	<p>Here’s the second argument again:</p>
	<div class="ct-quote"><strong>Argument 2:</strong> Modern society is vastly different from the societies that we inhabited during our evolutionary development. It stands to reason that those differences could mean we're missing out on some important things we used to have. Including in our diets. Fortunately, dietary supplements can provide things our modern environment is missing or has less of and that we would have gotten before. Therefore, shouldn't the average person now be able to add supplements to their diet that improve wellbeing and health, even without some specific health concern that they’re targeting?</div>
	<p>Notice that all this does is establish that we <em>could </em>be missing out on important components of nutrition. But plenty of things that <em>could </em>be true are actually false. In order for this to be a compelling argument for supplements, it would need to further establish that we actually <em>are </em>(or are likely to be) missing out, <em>and</em> that supplements can fill in the gap.</p>
	<p>It’s also worth noting that natural selection favors traits that increase <em>reproductive fitness </em>rather than optimizing maximum lifelong health. This means that speculation about the diets of our ancient ancestors could give us signals for things to investigate, but it does not entail that deviations from the behaviors of our ancient ancestors will have negative consequences.</p>
	<h2>What should you do?</h2>
	<p>We can’t tell you what to do (and we’re not medical professionals), but we can tell you how we read the evidence. It seems to us that if:</p>
	<ul><li>you don’t have a nutritional deficiency,</li><li>you’re not trying to treat or target a specific condition or context, and</li><li>you’re not a member of a demographic (e.g., vegans, elderly people) for whom specific targeted supplements are known to help,</li></ul>
	<p>then the empirical evidence does not support the claim that you need to be taking supplements. Further, there’s not enough evidence to warrant concluding that you’ll benefit from them. You might even be harmed.</p>
	<p>This is consistent with recommendations by medical professionals and organizations like the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.heart.org/en/healthy-living/healthy-eating/eat-smart/nutrition-basics/vitamin-supplements-hype-or-help-for-healthy-eating">American Heart Association</a> and the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.dietaryguidelines.gov/sites/default/files/2020-12/Dietary_Guidelines_for_Americans_2020-2025.pdf">US Department of Health and Human Services</a>, which prioritize nutrient-dense foods and targeted (rather than untargeted) use of supplements. The National Institutes of Health <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminC-HealthProfessional/">Office of Dietary Supplements</a><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminC-HealthProfessional/"> states</a>:</p>
	<div class="ct-quote ct-italic">“In general, a person’s nutritional needs should be met primarily through the diet, including fortified foods. Dietary supplements may be useful in cases where it is not possible to meet the needs for specific nutrients through food alone, especially during certain life stages.”</div>
	<p>Of course, we have mentioned that there are exceptions for targeting specific conditions or contexts. Here's a <em>partial</em> list of some of the most well-established examples. Note that none of these are generally recommended to everyone in the population.</p>
	<h3><strong><em>Some examples for specific groups of people:</em></strong></h3>
	<ul><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminB12-HealthProfessional/">Vitamin B12</a> for vegans and some vegetarians</li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminD-HealthProfessional/">Vitamin D</a> for people whose limited sunlight and dietary intake put them at risk of inadequate vitamin D, as well as individuals who have difficulty absorbing dietary fat, and people with diets low in vitamin D (including some vegans)</li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/lactose-intolerance/treatment">Lactase</a> tablets or drops to reduce symptoms of lactose intolerance for some lactose intolerant people</li></ul>
	<h3>Some examples for specific situations:</h3>
	<ul><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/folic-acid-for-the-prevention-of-neural-tube-defects-preventive-medication">Folic acid</a> shortly before and during pregnancy to prevent some of the most common birth defects</li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2018-099027">Creatine (monohydrate)</a> especially alongside resistance training, modestly improves strength and gains in <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1519/JSC.0000000000004862">lean body mass</a></li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00213-025-06775-1">Caffeine</a> to help with some aspects of short-term cognitive performance (though it's debated whether long-term use is overall helpful for cognitive performance once drug tolerance and withdrawal effects are factored in)</li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1503/cmaj.240570">Iron</a> for women experiencing heavy menstrual bleeding, after having iron levels tested to confirm low levels</li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.cd006095.pub5">Probiotics</a> may help with diarrhea related to taking antibiotics</li></ul>
	<p>These are not medical recommendations. If you want to explore more examples and figure out what’s best for you, then talking to a medical professional with relevant expertise is obviously a good place to start.</p>
	<p>Failing that, you might find <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://informationisbeautiful.net/visualizations/snake-oil-scientific-evidence-for-nutritional-supplements-vizsweet/">this infographic</a> a useful starting point. It lists many different things that supplements have been studied for treating, along with judgments about the strength of evidence (and if you want to dig even deeper, here’s their <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1VqJQ_C_R0OziW7m2wtdYz2-8n4_4xxyFRypzmo8iLN8/edit?gid=2#gid=2">spreadsheet containing much more information</a>). Though we do advise caution with this for a few reasons, chief among them being that (a) the infographic simplifies things more than we would,<sup>1</sup> and (b) some of the cited studies have been superseded.<sup>2</sup></p>
	<p>The infographic also illustrates the point we’re making in this article. In the image below, you can see the way the authors rated the strength of evidence for different supplements in 2010 (when the infographic was first made) compared to 2024 (the latest ratings). When put side-by-side like this, we can see that:</p>
	<ul><li>The number of things in the “strong” region has dropped off dramatically</li><li>Claims about “general health” have dropped out of the “strong” region over time</li></ul>
	<p>It’s also worth noting that, even in specific contexts where supplements <em>do</em> help, there are often established medical treatments (e.g., medications) known to be more effective. For example, you might take magnesium supplements to lower your blood pressure, but a standard-dose antihypertensive medication typically has substantially larger effects. This is another reason why discussion with a medical professional with relevant expertise is important.</p>
	<p>We hope that one day it will be discovered that some untargeted supplements have substantial positive benefits for the general population. Maybe that discovery will come, but that's not the way the evidence looks right now, despite a tremendous amount of research on the topic.</p>
	<h2><strong>How skeptical should you be?</strong></h2>
	<p>When you’re thinking about whether supplements are right for you, or you’re evaluating a claim made about a specific supplement, it’s worth remembering the following:</p>
	<p>1. In the US, supplements can enter stores without being checked for safety or efficacy by the FDA. In <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/fda-101-dietary-supplements#:~:text=The%20FDA%20does%20NOT%20have%20the%20authority%20to%20approve%20dietary%20supplements%20for%20safety%20and%20effectiveness%2C%20or%20to%20approve%20their%20labeling%2C%20before%20the%20supplements%20are%20sold%20to%20the%20public">the FDA’s own words</a>:</p>
	<div class="ct-quote ct-italic">“The FDA does NOT have the authority to approve dietary supplements for safety and effectiveness, or to approve their labeling, before the supplements are sold to the public.”</div>
	<p>This doesn’t mean they are completely unregulated, but they are much less regulated than in some other countries. So, remember (in the US in particular) <strong>the fact that a supplement is sold in a store does not mean it has been found to be safe or effective.</strong></p>
	<p>2. When someone gives you an argument about health (such as the ones at the start of this article) that points to mechanisms in the body and does <em>not</em> point to empirical data that <em>directly</em> establishes their claim, they’re engaging in what’s called “mechanistic reasoning”. <strong>Arguments relying on mechanistic reasoning can provide some evidence, but <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.clearerthinking.org/post/how-to-tell-strong-evidence-from-weak-evidence">it’s generally among the weakest forms of evidence</a></strong>:</p>

*image: https://beehiiv-images-production.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/asset/file/869e7211-421c-4444-aac4-9f110811b391/d7c802_0d521f35e6144b689cf35a9b108443a8_mv2.png
*html
	<p class="ct-caption"><em>(Source: <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="link" href="https://www.clearerthinking.org/post/how-to-tell-strong-evidence-from-weak-evidence">Our article about the Hierarchy of Evidence</a>)</em></p>

*html
	<p>3. Even when an ingredient <em>is</em> genuinely found to have a positive effect, there’s more to consider. For example: <strong>Have the benefits been shown for people relevantly similar to you? Are the benefits worth the price, and do they outweigh the risks?</strong></p>
	<p>And, finally:</p>
	<p>4. <strong>The fact that your body needs something in order to survive does not entail that you will benefit from having more of it.</strong></p>
	<p>If you found this article interesting, you might enjoy our free <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://programs.clearerthinking.org/can_you_detect_weak_arguments.html">“Can You Detect Weak Arguments” quiz</a>. Find out how well you can spot misleading rhetoric and recognize your own bias using real-world examples.</p>
	<p class="ct-btnrow"><a class="ct-cta" href="https://programs.clearerthinking.org/can_you_detect_weak_arguments.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Launch the “Can You Detect Weak Arguments?” quiz!</a></p>
	<h2><strong>Footnotes</strong></h2>
	<p><sup><strong>1</strong></sup><strong> The infographic simplifies things more than we would:</strong> For example, it states that <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD007470.pub3">this study</a> “suggests that vitamin D3 may reduce mortality” (without specifying a specific population). Taken at face value, this might seem to suggest that vitamin D supplementation for the general population could be beneficial (contrary to what we’re saying the evidence shows). However, that study includes 26 trials using participants with vitamin D insufficiency and 19 trials using participants with adequate vitamin D. The authors of the study note that “Vitamin D had no statistically significant effect on mortality in the trials including participants with vitamin D adequacy” and “The effect of vitamin D3 on participants with adequate vitamin D status is unknown.”</p>
	<p><sup><strong>2</strong></sup><strong> Some of the cited studies have been superseded:</strong> For example, that same study of vitamin D supplementation included trials up to February 2012, but more recent evidence undermines the generalized claim the infographic makes. For example, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://doi.org/10.1210/clinem/dgae312">a recent systematic review</a> found that “there was no evidence to support empiric vitamin D supplementation [...] in healthy adults aged 19 to 75” (but it did find some benefits for people aged 75+ and those with high-risk prediabetes).</p>
	<p><strong><em>Note:</em></strong><em> The authors of this article are not medical professionals and this article is not intended to constitute medical advice. </em></p>

